+# Float-model certification boundary
+
+UORust's Prelude models IEEE values as bit patterns and computes with mathematical
+naturals. The production `rust-std` target represents `Nat` as checked `u64`.
+Certifying a program that reports integer overflow does not certify that it
+returns the original Rust floating-point result.
+
+For example, `floatMul 11 52` on two binary64 `1.0` values multiplies their
+significands, `2^52 * 2^52`. That intermediate needs 105 bits although the final
+IEEE result is exactly `1.0`. Two maximal normal significands require 106 bits.
+The prelude differential evaluator uses arbitrary-size natural numbers, so a
+passing host-float comparison does not establish production executability.
+
+UE-20 therefore refuses any production root whose transitive closure reaches a
+`Prelude::float*` definition, with the stable cause prefix
+`unbounded-float-model:` in `refusals.toml`, keyed by the root's kappa. Such roots
+are omitted from `request.json`, including batch requests. Unrelated integer
+roots remain eligible. The mathematical definitions remain available to proofs.
+This conservative guard does not claim that every float helper is intrinsically
+unsafe; it states that the current export path has no machine-width evidence
+with which to admit the closure.
+
+The test `float_model_intermediates_exceed_the_production_nat_width_ue_20`
+executes the actual Prelude with the existing arbitrary-size evaluator and
+records the largest natural value's bit length. It is model evidence, not
+compiled IEEE replay. Known examples include binary64 multiplication (105/106),
+division (160), fused multiply-add (105), square-root search (325), and exponent
+alignment in addition (103). Binary32 division (73) and square-root search
+(153) also exceed 64 bits. Replacing `u64` with `u128` alone cannot cover these
+algorithms.
+
+Removing the guard requires a bounded representation and a preservation
+argument for all reachable intermediates, or checked multi-limb operations;
+compiled native and Wasm replay of the actual exported float closure; explicit
+NaN, signed-zero, subnormal, rounding and overflow behavior; and differential
+vectors covering boundary values and operation ordering. A new bound must be
+carried by source-owned evidence, not inferred from a function's name or a
+successful proof of an unbounded model. The ordinary complete acceptance gate
+remains `just vv` under the repository's pinned SDK.
+
+To reproduce the narrow production boundary in a pinned SDK, run
+`bash scripts/probe-numeric-export.sh`. It keeps its output directory, exports
+the exact multiplication primitive, replays the certificate bindings and then
+runs native and import-free Wasm controls. It does not claim that the full
+IEEE `floatMul` function was exported. The fixture and commands were exercised
+in stages for the initial investigation; the portable shell wrapper has also
+been syntax-checked.
+
+The guard inspects each root's reachable definition closure, not the set of
+loaded modules. Tests load the entire unused float Prelude alongside an
+integer root and a bit-identity root, which stay eligible; direct calls,
+unqualified and qualified Behavior aliases, and a non-float-named Prelude
+alias that reaches floatMul are refused. It blocks reachable float helpers
+for add/sub/mul/div/remainder, sqrt/FMA, rounding/conversions, comparisons,
+classification, negation, abs and copy-sign. This is conservative: no claim
+is made that every fixed-format comparison or sign helper needs wide math.
+
+The MIR to_bits/from_bits branch (`mir/lower.rs`, `Kind::Bits`) returns the
+input bit-pattern term unchanged. A root made only of that identity and no
+reachable float helper is not refused by this guard; the usual compiler
+eligibility checks still apply. This does not independently establish the
+correctness of bitcasts or memory representation. In contrast, floatToNat
+with width 64 eagerly computes `2^64 - 1` even for input 1.0; the regression
+observes the 65-bit intermediate although the final result is only 1.
